>
  <h1>Music</h1>
  <p>
    I've been chasing music my entire life and I'm still a beginner at all
    of it. The talent fairy did not visit. The practice plan is what I have.
  </p>

  <h2>Playing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Guitar</strong> — chord work and fingerpicking, building the daily-practice habit before any actual technique.</li>
    <li><strong>Piano</strong> — back to fundamentals. Scales, intervals, the things you skip when you teach yourself the first time.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Learning</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Music production</strong> — slowly working through the signal chain and the discipline of finishing tracks instead of starting them. This is the hardest one. Producers know what I mean.</li>
    <li><strong>DJing</strong> — beatmatching, library work, the long road to clean transitions and reading a room.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Listening</h2>
  <p style="color: #888;">
    This section is honestly under construction. If you and I share a
    playlist instinct, send me what you've been on lately and I'll do the
    same — <a href="mailto:vishvaddi@gmail.com">vishvaddi@gmail.com</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Why music sits on the same site as estimating</h2>
  <p>
    Because rhythm, structure, and the discipline of practising imperfectly
    every day all transfer. Music is the format I trust most for the kind of
    long, slow, low-ego skill-building that doesn't come naturally to me. It
    keeps the rest of the system honest.
  </p>
